  "content": {
    "id": "v2-0beaf-p47-practice-slot-22-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Find all values of $k$ for which $(k-3)x^2+2x+1=0$ has 2 real solutions.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$k<4$, with $k\\ne3$. Two distinct real solutions require $\\Delta>0$ and a nonzero quadratic coefficient.",
      "worked": "For a quadratic equation, $k-3\\ne0$, so $k\\ne3$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\Delta &= 2^2-4(k-3)(1)\\\\\n&=16-4k.\n\\end{align*}$$\nTwo distinct real solutions require $\\Delta>0$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\n16-4k &>0\\\\\n-4k &>-16\\\\\nk &<4.\n\\end{align*}$$\nWhen $k=3$, the equation becomes $2x+1=0$, which has only one solution. Therefore $k<4$, with $k\\ne3$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": null
  },
